    {
        "content": "<p>This is well documented somewhere, but I don't recall where. One issue is that your theorems are no longer about <code>?a + ?b</code> but <code> ?f ?a ?b</code>, which is much harder to deal with in practice. And there don't seem to be many practical reasons to do it the way you're suggesting.</p>",
        "id": 613475428,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruben Van de Velde",
        "timestamp": 1785345319
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I'm not suggesting it be done this way. I'm just hoping to benefit from the community's hindsight before I make things harder for myself than they need to be.</p>",
        "id": 613476712,
        "sender_full_name": "aerh",
        "timestamp": 1785345640
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't know how this was originally designed, but I find Mathlib's design natural when considered as an evolution from existing programming practice. The definition of operators like add and mul are naturally carried by the type itself (known as operator overloading), and the math content is proving monoid axioms on top of it.</p>",
        "id": 613479864,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1785346508
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Attaching <code>add</code> and <code>mul</code> to types makes sense. My question is more about how and why the math content is structured the way it is. It may just be a matter of programming convenience in the end, and I have no problem with that.</p>",
        "id": 613484924,
        "sender_full_name": "aerh",
        "timestamp": 1785347980
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Because as soon as you have a commutative op <code>x @ y</code>, then the op homs <code>f, g</code> on your space have two operations on them, either pointwise op <code>f @ g = f x @ g x</code>, or composition <code>f × g = g(f(x)</code> and one distributes over the other.</p>",
        "id": 613492041,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1785350154
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As soon as you have structures like <code>Ring</code>, you need to have two different <code>Monoid</code> instances on a single type - the additive one and the multiplicative one. The way I understand things, the typeclass resolution system is not designed (possibly, could not be better designed) to deal with this, so Mathlib has two monoid structures: <code>AddMonoid</code> for additionally-noted monoids, and <code>Monoid</code> for multiplicatively-noted monoids. And then, since the <code>+</code> and <code>*</code> notations are attached (through the <code>Add</code> and <code>Mul</code>classes) to <code>AddMonoid</code> and <code>Monoid</code> by default, this means you can write complex algebraic expressions in a very natural way if you're at all used to them in pen-and-paper mathematics.</p>\n<p>But, while working with just monoids, this means you have a single theory of monoids. Well, actually you have two, one for <code>Monoid</code> and one for <code>AddMonoid</code>, with theorems duplicated between the two - but not proofs; if you have a look at the Mathlib source code, it typically only states the multiplicative versions, and there is some black magic at work that generates the additive version.</p>\n<p>I <em>think</em> this is all explained in <em>Mathematics in Lean</em>, except for the \"to additive\" part which I have not seen explained in the documentation I've read.</p>",
        "id": 613598431,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1785401609
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you for the replies. They've given me a lot to think about and experiment with. I had the impression that the resolution problem was only about type classes, not instances. Oh well.</p>",
        "id": 613693478,
        "sender_full_name": "aerh",
        "timestamp": 1785430725
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note, I may well have the terminology wrong. \"Instance\" in this (or at least in my mind) is the specific instance of <code>Monoid T</code> that applies in your case - so it is a specific term of a given type class.</p>",
        "id": 613740637,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1785445689
    }
]